 into machine registers.\n   120\t\n   121\t   Two entry shapes carry more than registers:\n   122\t\n   123\t   - A `:request-sent` entry is a writer whose retained FFI request has now\n   124\t     been appended. The call starts waiting for its correlated response,\n   125\t     exactly as an immediately-sent one does, so the entry is replaced by its\n   126\t     response reader and the machine stays blocked.\n   127\t   - A `:call-id` entry is a response reader. The woken value is a response\n   128\t     envelope, so `ffi/call-result` unwraps it, checks correlation, and the\n   129\t     parked call leaves `:parked` rather than accumulating.\"\n   130\t  ([base entry] (semantic-restore base entry (:value entry)))\n   131\t  ([base entry val]\n   132\t   (if (:request-sent entry)\n   133\t     (assoc base\n   134\t            :wait-set (conj (vec (or (:wait-set base) []))\n   135\t                            (response-wait-entry (:segment entry)\n   136\t                                                 (:pc entry)\n   137\t                                                 (:env entry)\n   138\t                                                 (:stack entry)\n   139\t                                                 (:k entry)\n   140\t                                                 (:call-id entry)))\n   141\t            :control nil\n   142\t            :k nil\n   143\t            :value :yin/blocked\n   144\t            :blocked? true\n   145\t            :halted? false)\n   146\t     (let [call-id (:call-id entry)\n   147\t           base (if call-id (update base :parked dissoc call-id) base)\n   148\t           val (if call-id (ffi/call-result val call-id) val)]\n   149\t       (put-registers base\n   150\t                      (:segment entry)\n   151\t                      (:pc entry)\n   152\t                      val\n   153\t                      (:stack entry)\n   154\t                      (:env entry)\n   155\t                      (:k entry))))))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:475:(def call-in-stream-key\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:480:(def call-out-stream-key\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:485:(def call-out-cursor-key\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:2043:                   {call-in-stream-key call-in,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:2044:                    call-out-stream-key call-out,\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:2045:                    call-out-cursor-key (cursor-entry call-out-stream-key\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:38:  (let [in (get resources vm/call-in-stream-key)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:39:        out (get resources vm/call-out-stream-key)]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:61:   :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id vm/call-out-cursor-key},\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:63:   :stream-id vm/call-out-stream-key})\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:84:             :cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref, :id vm/call-out-cursor-key}\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:85:             :stream-id vm/call-out-stream-key)))\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:173:  (let [call-out (get (:resources vm) vm/call-out-stream-key)\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:215:      (let [call-in (get (:resources vm) vm/call-in-stream-key)\nThe route-cell routing fix (P1 from gate r3).** The wholesale replacement of the receiver's fixed call-pair keys is gone, and the outstanding call now routes to the emitter's pair entirely through non-fixed state:\n\n- Deleted `call-pair-resources` outright — `lower-frame` (src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:712-716) no longer installs the emitter's reflected call streams at `vm/call-in-stream-key`/`vm/call-out-stream-key`. Those keys remain the receiving composition's own supply (`ffi/call-pair`, ffi.cljc:33-40), so the resumed program's future FFI calls use the receiver's own pair (UCF §\"Outstanding calls route to the emitter's pair; the resumer's pair is its own\", yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:614-624).\n- The lowered `:request-sent` entry's `:stream-id` is the fresh reflection key of the emitter's own call-in (remote.cljc:648) — the retry appends on the emitter's pair without occupying the fixed call-in key (remote.cljc:641-661).\n- The route cell lowers as the fixed `vm/call-out-cursor-key` entry — the one key the restored response wait reads (`semantic-restore` → `response-wait-entry`, semantic.cljc:106-115) — still naming the emitter's own call-out reflection via its fresh key, not `:yin/call-out` (`cell-resources`, remote.cljc:524-543; `cursor-entry-key`, remote.cljc:556-562 keeps §7.5.3 sharing for co-waiters). Confirmed r2 behavior (link waits on the fixed link keys, remote.cljc:665-668 + `link-pair-resources` remote.cljc:565) is untouched.\n\n**Test extended past the migrated call** (test/yin/vm/ucf/remote_test.cljc:507-618, `a-retained-ffi-request-migrates-and-the-retry-resumes`): the program makes two calls — the migrated one and a new one. After the migrated call's response reaches its waiter through the emitter's pair (retry envelope verified landing verbatim on the emitter's call-in via the wire; response consumed via the route cell), the program's new call flows on the receiver's own pair: its request lands on the receiver's own call-in (`[\"world\"]`, :606-611) and nothing crosses to the emitter's call-in; the receiver's own bridge answers on its own call-out, and after the composition re-points its spent route at its own pair (the doc's separate-stores rule, :561-565), run 3 halts with \"world!\" and an empty wait-set. The routing assertions (:545-559) pin: no fixed stream keys in the lowered table, retry on the emitter's reflection, route cell naming the emitter's call-out. Debugging used a /tmp scratch script (deleted) — no repo files beyond the two slice-8 files.\n\n**Lane counts (focused, exact).**\n- JVM `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.ucf.remote-test`: Ran 18 tests containing 162 assertions — 0 failures, 0 errors.\n- Node (shadow-cljs build; `node target/node-tests.js --test=yin.vm.ucf.remote-test`): Ran 18 tests containing 162 assertions — 0 failures, 0 errors.\n- Dart (`flutter test test/cljd-out/yin/vm/ucf/remote-test_test.dart